This is perhaps the most profound and poetic application of the entire theory.

47:20.120 --> 47:23.820
Section 7 introduces a computational framework called TARTAN.

47:24.000 --> 47:27.960
TARTAN. Trajectory wear. Recursive tiling with annotated noise.

47:28.320 --> 47:33.060
It sounds intensely complex, but the underlying concept is deeply elegant.

47:33.760 --> 47:38.520
We are looking at a study by Leigh et al. on paleoclimate reconstruction.

47:38.840 --> 47:40.220
Paleoclimate reconstruction.

47:40.400 --> 47:45.960
Specifically, they are using rhodoliths, which are a type of hard coralline algae,

47:46.240 --> 47:49.260
to reconstruct the ancient temperatures of the Red Sea.

47:49.260 --> 47:55.280
Let me set the stage for you, the listener, because the scientific setup here is like a massive global detective story.

47:56.120 --> 48:03.700
Climatologists wanted to know the exact daily temperature of the ocean water in the Red Sea from thousands of years ago.

48:03.820 --> 48:04.900
Which is a tall order.

48:05.200 --> 48:08.020
Obviously, humanity didn't have thermometers in the water back then.

48:08.340 --> 48:14.380
But these algae, these rhodoliths, they grow slowly over time in microscopic layers, almost exactly like tree rings.

48:14.520 --> 48:15.600
Right, banding patterns.

48:15.600 --> 48:23.780
And as they grow, the chemical makeup of each tiny calcified band physically traps the temperature data of the water for that specific day.

48:23.920 --> 48:28.400
They are essentially perfect, naturally occurring biological hard drives.

48:28.540 --> 48:35.000
Right. They are recording the data flawlessly, except there is one massive, seemingly insurmountable problem.

48:35.100 --> 48:35.720
I don't live forever.

48:35.720 --> 48:40.060
Right. No single algae lives for thousands of years.

48:40.360 --> 48:43.500
A single rhodolith might only live for a few decades.

48:44.120 --> 48:48.740
So you don't have one continuous, unbroken record stretching back into antiquity.

48:48.760 --> 48:49.820
You just have fragments.

48:50.180 --> 48:55.920
You just have a messy, chaotic pile of thousands of fragmented, broken, overlapping partial records.

48:55.920 --> 49:06.700
How on earth do you construct a massive, mathematically rigorous historical timeline when your hard drive is shattered into 10,000 disconnected pieces?

49:06.700 --> 49:17.520
Well, if you approach this using a traditional localizationist view of memory, the idea that a memory must be stored sequentially in one discrete, continuous location, you can't solve it.

49:17.700 --> 49:19.820
The data is hopelessly lost to time.

49:19.880 --> 49:21.240
It's just a pile of garbage data.

49:21.240 --> 49:29.680
But Flickshanine explains how the researchers solved this using a mathematical technique called dynamic time warping, which the Tartan framework formalizes perfectly.

49:29.900 --> 49:30.960
Dynamic time warping.

49:31.200 --> 49:37.180
They realized they had to treat memory not as a single localized file, but as a distributed trajectory residue.

49:37.440 --> 49:38.800
Distributed trajectory residue.

49:38.880 --> 49:42.880
Okay, I need you to unpack that because dynamic time warping sounds like literal time travel.

49:42.900 --> 49:44.060
I know, it sounds like sci-fi.

49:44.340 --> 49:47.820
How does DTW actually work to align these fragments?

49:47.820 --> 49:53.060
Think of each fragmented algae record as a single tile in a massive timeline.

49:53.280 --> 49:54.240
Okay, a tile.

49:54.480 --> 50:00.260
No, you can't just line them up end to end because you don't know exactly what year any specific tile started growing.

50:00.460 --> 50:01.480
Right, there's no time step.

50:01.620 --> 50:02.000
Exactly.

50:02.400 --> 50:09.880
And you can't just match them point by point because one algae might have grown slightly faster in the summer than another, stretching its data out.

50:10.000 --> 50:11.040
The scales are different.

50:11.260 --> 50:13.360
This is where dynamic time warping comes in.

50:13.360 --> 50:20.240
DTW is an algorithm that measures the similarity between two sequences that may vary in speed.

50:20.660 --> 50:27.500
It mathematically stretches and squashes the time axis of the data fragments to find the underlying structural similarities.

